<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Winora.App</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <!--
      The icon Windows shows for the file, the taskbar and Alt+Tab.

      The packaged build gets these from the tiles the manifest declares, so nothing here was ever
      missing while MSIX was the only way to run Winora. An unpackaged .exe has no manifest to read
      them from and falls back to the generic .NET icon — which is what the portable build shipped
      with until 2026-08-24. Built from Square150x150Logo at nine sizes down to 16 px, because
      Windows picks per surface and a single large image is resampled badly in the small ones.
    -->
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\Winora.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <DefaultLanguage>ru-RU</DefaultLanguage>
    <!--
      The Windows App SDK DeploymentManager auto-initializer runs from a static constructor and
      activates a WinRT type before the runtime is reachable, throwing REGDB_E_CLASSNOTREG. A throw
      in a .cctor kills the process instantly with no window, no Windows Error Reporting entry, and
      no chance for any handler to record it. A packaged app declares a framework dependency, so the
      OS guarantees the runtime and this initializer has nothing to do.
    -->
    <WindowsAppSdkDeploymentManagerInitialize>false</WindowsAppSdkDeploymentManagerInitialize>
    <WinUISDKReferences>false</WinUISDKReferences>
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    The portable build: one .exe to hand to somebody who has nothing installed.

    Switched on with -p:WinoraPortable=true so the MSIX build above is untouched. The two cannot be
    the same build: MSIX declares a framework dependency and lets the OS supply the Windows App SDK,
    which is exactly what a friend with a bare machine does not have.

    WindowsAppSDKSelfContained puts the SDK's own binaries in the output, which is why the
    DeploymentManager initializer above can stay off: there is no runtime to go looking for.

    IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract is the load-bearing one. WinUI keeps compiled XAML in
    resources.pri, not in the assembly, so an .exe that bundles only managed code starts and then
    fails to load its own windows. With this set the host unpacks everything — pri, native DLLs, the
    lot — into a temp folder before any of it runs. The cost is a slow first launch while a few
    hundred megabytes are written; afterwards the folder is reused.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(WinoraPortable)' == 'true'">
    <WindowsPackageType>None</WindowsPackageType>
    <!--
      The released file is named here, not renamed afterwards.

      WinUI keeps compiled XAML in resources.pri and resolves the resource map by the running
      module's name. Renaming the published file breaks that lookup: the window's own markup is
      never found and the process dies at startup with a stowed WinRT exception (0xC000027B) before
      anything can report it. Measured on 2026-08-24 — identical bytes named Winora.exe, Winora2.exe
      and WinoraApp.exe all died; only Winora.App.exe, the name it was built under, started.

      The release workflow used to do that rename, which means every published build would have
      failed to start on every machine. Building under the final name removes the rename entirely
      and keeps the assembly, the resource map and the file agreeing with each other.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>Winora</AssemblyName>
    <!--
      Still true, and not a leftover: the SDK's single-file targets refuse to build without it,
      because the MSIX tooling is what generates the resources.pri they then embed.
    -->
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
    <!--
      Points the App SDK at the folder the host extracted into. Without it the app looks for its
      runtime beside the .exe, where nothing was unpacked, and registration-free WinRT activation
      fails on the first XAML type.
    -->
    <WindowsAppSdkUndockedRegFreeWinRTInitialize>true</WindowsAppSdkUndockedRegFreeWinRTInitialize>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>true</WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract>true</IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract>
    <EnableCompressionInSingleFile>true</EnableCompressionInSingleFile>
    <!--
      Trimming is off on purpose. WinUI resolves types by name out of XAML, so the trimmer cannot
      see who uses what and removes controls that are only ever named in markup. The failure is a
      blank window at runtime, not an error at build time.
    -->
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <!--
      AppPackages holds the MSIX builds, one folder per version, and it is nobody's business but the
      MSIX build's. Turning on the MSIX tooling above — which the single-file targets insist on —
      also brings its item globs, and those sweep that folder up as content to publish. Left alone
      the portable .exe came out at 1.5 GB, of which 1.35 GB was 150 copies of previous releases of
      itself. Excluded here rather than deleted: the folder is already ignored by git, and clearing
      it out is the owner's call, not the build's.
    -->
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);AppPackages\**;Assets.backup-*\**</DefaultItemExcludes>
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>ru-RU;en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" />
    <PackageReference Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Winora.Core\Winora.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Winora.Infrastructure\Winora.Infrastructure.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Winora.System\Winora.System.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="Assets\SplashScreen.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\LockScreenLogo.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Square150x150Logo.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Square44x44Logo.scale-200.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Square44x44Logo.targetsize-24_altform-unplated.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\StoreLogo.png" />
    <Content Include="Assets\Wide310x150Logo.scale-200.png" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Manifest Include="$(ApplicationManifest)" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(DisableMsixProjectCapabilityAddedByProject)' != 'true' and '$(EnableMsixTooling)' == 'true'">
    <ProjectCapability Include="Msix" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(DisableHasPackageAndPublishMenuAddedByProject)' != 'true' and '$(EnableMsixTooling)' == 'true'">
    <HasPackageAndPublishMenu>true</HasPackageAndPublishMenu>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
